(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-4-endo-iii-exam2-female-pregnancy-lactation"]={"title":"Endocrine III — Female Reproductive, Pregnancy & Lactation (90 Questions)","category":"Physiology","questions":[{"q":"Which hypothalamic hormone initiates the female reproductive cycle?","choices":["GHRH","GnRH","CRH","TRH"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: GnRH from the hypothalamus initiates the female cycle by stimulating pituitary LH and FSH release, just as in males. — Endocrine III — Female Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"GnRH directly stimulates release of:","choices":["Estrogen and progesterone","hCG","Oxytocin and prolactin","LH and FSH"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: GnRH acts on the anterior pituitary to release the gonadotropins LH and FSH; estrogen/progesterone are downstream ovarian products. — Endocrine III — Female Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"FSH primarily stimulates:","choices":["Granulosa cells","Corpus luteum","Leydig cells","Placenta"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: FSH stimulates granulosa cells of the ovarian follicle to grow and produce estrogen; LH acts more on theca cells and triggers ovulation. — Endocrine III — Female Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"LH primarily stimulates:","choices":["Oxytocin secretion","Granulosa cells only","Milk production","Ovulation and theca cell function"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: LH triggers ovulation and stimulates theca cell androgen production; granulosa cells are primarily FSH-responsive. — Endocrine III — Female Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Granulosa cells primarily produce:","choices":["Estrogen","Cortisol","Testosterone","hCG"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: Granulosa cells aromatize theca-derived androgens into estrogen — the dominant follicular-phase hormone. — Endocrine III — Female Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Theca cells primarily synthesize:","choices":["Progesterone only","Prolactin","Oxytocin","Androgens"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: Theca cells synthesize androgens (under LH), which granulosa cells then convert to estrogen — the basis of the two-cell model. — Endocrine III — Female Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Which enzyme converts androgens into estrogen within granulosa cells?","choices":["ACE","Aromatase","Deiodinase","5α-reductase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: Aromatase in granulosa cells converts androgens to estrogen. 5α-reductase makes DHT; neither deiodinase nor ACE is involved here. — Endocrine III — Female Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"The \"two-cell, two-gonadotropin\" model refers to:","choices":["Hypothalamus and pituitary","Sertoli and Leydig cells","Theca and granulosa cells working together under LH and FSH","Placenta and corpus luteum"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: The two-cell, two-gonadotropin model — theca cells (LH) make androgens, granulosa cells (FSH) aromatize them to estrogen — the two cells cooperate. — Endocrine III — Female Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"During the follicular phase, the dominant ovarian hormone is:","choices":["Estrogen","Progesterone","hCG","Testosterone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: Estrogen dominates the follicular phase, driving endometrial proliferation and the eventual LH surge. Progesterone dominates the luteal phase. — Endocrine III — Female Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"The purpose of the follicular phase is primarily to:","choices":["Maintain pregnancy","Mature a dominant follicle","Form the placenta","Produce prolactin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: The follicular phase exists to mature a dominant follicle for ovulation. Pregnancy maintenance and placenta formation come later. — Endocrine III — Female Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Unlike males, oogonia complete most mitotic divisions:","choices":["Before birth","During menopause","After puberty","After fertilization"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: Oogonia complete most of their mitotic proliferation before birth; a female is born with her lifetime supply of primary oocytes arrested in meiosis I. — Endocrine III — Female Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Each primary oocyte ultimately produces:","choices":["One mature ovum","Four ova","Eight ova","Two ova"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: Each primary oocyte yields one mature ovum (the other products become polar bodies), unlike spermatogenesis, which yields four sperm. — Endocrine III — Female Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Completion of Meiosis II occurs:","choices":["Before ovulation","At menopause","Only if fertilization occurs","During fetal life"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: Meiosis II completes only if fertilization occurs; the secondary oocyte is ovulated arrested in metaphase II until a sperm penetrates it. — Endocrine III — Female Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Ovulation is triggered primarily by:","choices":["LH surge","Progesterone surge","Estrogen withdrawal","FSH surge"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: The mid-cycle LH surge is the direct trigger for ovulation. FSH rises too, but LH is the ovulatory signal. — Endocrine III — Female Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Ovulation typically occurs around:","choices":["Day 1","Day 7","Day 14","Day 28"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: In a typical 28-day cycle, ovulation occurs around day 14, roughly midway, driven by the LH surge. — Endocrine III — Female Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"The follicle remaining after ovulation becomes the:","choices":["Corpus luteum","Morula","Corpus albicans","Blastocyst"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: After ovulation the ruptured follicle luteinizes into the corpus luteum, which secretes progesterone. The corpus albicans is its later degenerated remnant. — Endocrine III — Female Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"The corpus luteum primarily secretes:","choices":["hCG","Testosterone","Estrogen only","Progesterone (and some estrogen)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: The corpus luteum mainly secretes progesterone (plus some estrogen) to maintain the secretory endometrium during the luteal phase. — Endocrine III — Female Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Without fertilization, the corpus luteum:","choices":["Continues growing","Becomes the placenta","Produces increasing progesterone indefinitely","Degenerates after approximately 12 days"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: Without fertilization the corpus luteum regresses after about 12 days, causing progesterone/estrogen to fall and triggering menstruation. — Endocrine III — Female Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Loss of the corpus luteum causes:","choices":["Decreased estrogen and progesterone","Increased hCG","Increased oxytocin","Increased progesterone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: Corpus luteum loss removes its hormone output, so estrogen and progesterone both fall — the trigger for menstruation. — Endocrine III — Female Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"The fall in estrogen and progesterone ultimately results in:","choices":["Fertilization","Implantation","Ovulation","Menstruation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: Falling estrogen and progesterone cause the endometrium to shed — menstruation. Their withdrawal is the direct menstrual trigger. — Endocrine III — Female Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"The average menstrual cycle lasts approximately:","choices":["28 days","14 days","35 days","21 days"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: The average menstrual cycle is about 28 days, measured from the first day of one period to the next. — Endocrine III — Female Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"The primary goal of the menstrual cycle is:","choices":["Produce progesterone continuously","Prevent ovulation","Continuous estrogen production","Release one viable ovum and prepare the uterus for implantation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: The cycle's purpose is to release one viable ovum and prepare the uterine lining for possible implantation. — Endocrine III — Female Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"During the proliferative (estrogen) phase, the endometrium:","choices":["Detaches from the uterus","Thickens due to cellular proliferation","Produces hCG","Degenerates"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: During the proliferative (estrogen) phase, the endometrium thickens through cellular proliferation, rebuilding after menstruation. — Endocrine III — Female Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Progesterone transforms the endometrium into a:","choices":["Keratinized layer","Calcified layer","Fibrotic layer","Secretory endometrium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: Progesterone converts the proliferated lining into a secretory endometrium, rich in glycogen-secreting glands ready to support an embryo. — Endocrine III — Female Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Which hormone decreases uterine contractions during the luteal phase?","choices":["Oxytocin","Prolactin","Estrogen","Progesterone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: Progesterone quiets the myometrium, decreasing uterine contractions during the luteal phase to protect a potential pregnancy. — Endocrine III — Female Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Positive feedback occurs when rising estrogen causes:","choices":["LH surge","Suppression of LH","Increased progesterone only","Suppression of GnRH"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: Late-follicular high estrogen switches to positive feedback, causing the LH surge that triggers ovulation — the key exception to estrogen's usual negative feedback. — Endocrine III — Female Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Which phase immediately follows ovulation?","choices":["Proliferative phase","Follicular phase","Menstrual phase","Luteal phase"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: The luteal phase immediately follows ovulation, when the corpus luteum forms and secretes progesterone. (Proliferative/follicular precede ovulation.) — Endocrine III — Female Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"The major source of progesterone during the luteal phase is the:","choices":["Anterior pituitary","Placenta","Corpus luteum","Hypothalamus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: The corpus luteum is the major progesterone source during the luteal phase, sustaining the secretory endometrium. — Endocrine III — Female Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"A woman has an absent LH surge. Which event is MOST likely to fail?","choices":["Menstruation","FSH secretion","Ovulation","Endometrial proliferation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: An absent LH surge means ovulation fails, since the surge is the direct ovulatory trigger. Endometrial proliferation (estrogen-driven) can still occur. — Endocrine III — Female Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"A woman has a normal follicular phase but her corpus luteum fails to develop. Which finding is MOST likely?","choices":["Increased hCG production","Elevated progesterone","Sustained pregnancy","Failure of the secretory endometrium to develop"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: If the corpus luteum fails to develop, there is no progesterone to build the secretory endometrium — so the secretory transformation fails. — Endocrine III — Female Reproductive Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Fertilization normally occurs in the:","choices":["Cervix","Ampulla of the fallopian tube","Uterus","Ovary"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: Fertilization normally occurs in the ampulla, the widened distal portion of the fallopian tube, not in the uterus or ovary. — Endocrine III — Pregnancy & Placental Physiology lecture"},{"q":"The first cell formed after fertilization is the:","choices":["Blastocyst","Gastrula","Zygote","Morula"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: The zygote is the first single cell formed when sperm and egg fuse; it then cleaves into the morula and later the blastocyst. — Endocrine III — Pregnancy & Placental Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Implantation normally begins approximately:","choices":["21 days after fertilization","6-7 days after fertilization","3 days after fertilization","24 hours after fertilization"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: Implantation of the blastocyst into the endometrium begins about 6–7 days after fertilization. — Endocrine III — Pregnancy & Placental Physiology lecture"},{"q":"The structure that implants into the endometrium is the:","choices":["Morula","Primary oocyte","Corpus luteum","Blastocyst"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: The blastocyst — with its outer trophoblast — is the stage that implants into the endometrium. The morula precedes it. — Endocrine III — Pregnancy & Placental Physiology lecture"},{"q":"The trophoblast ultimately contributes to formation of the:","choices":["Pituitary gland","Ovary","Corpus luteum","Placenta"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: The trophoblast (outer blastocyst layer) develops into the placenta and fetal membranes. — Endocrine III — Pregnancy & Placental Physiology lecture"},{"q":"The trophoblast differentiates into:","choices":["Cytotrophoblast and syncytiotrophoblast","Endometrium and myometrium","Chorion and amnion only","Granulosa and theca cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: The trophoblast differentiates into the inner cytotrophoblast and outer syncytiotrophoblast, the latter invading the endometrium and making hCG. — Endocrine III — Pregnancy & Placental Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Which trophoblastic layer secretes hCG?","choices":["Cytotrophoblast","Corpus luteum","Endometrium","Syncytiotrophoblast"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: The syncytiotrophoblast secretes hCG, which rescues the corpus luteum in early pregnancy. — Endocrine III — Pregnancy & Placental Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) primarily functions to:","choices":["Maintain the corpus luteum","Produce estrogen directly","Trigger ovulation","Stimulate oxytocin release"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: hCG maintains the corpus luteum so it keeps producing progesterone until the placenta takes over — it mimics LH. — Endocrine III — Pregnancy & Placental Physiology lecture"},{"q":"If hCG were absent during early pregnancy, the MOST likely consequence would be:","choices":["Increased prolactin","Degeneration of the corpus luteum","Increased estrogen","Increased progesterone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: Without hCG the corpus luteum would degenerate, progesterone would fall, and the pregnancy would likely be lost. — Endocrine III — Pregnancy & Placental Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Pregnancy tests detect:","choices":["hCG","Estrogen","Progesterone","Relaxin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: Pregnancy tests detect hCG, produced by the syncytiotrophoblast and detectable in blood/urine shortly after implantation. — Endocrine III — Pregnancy & Placental Physiology lecture"},{"q":"The placenta functions primarily in:","choices":["Gas exchange","All of the above","Nutrient transfer","Waste removal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: The placenta serves gas exchange, nutrient transfer, and waste removal simultaneously — all of the above — acting as the fetal lung, gut, and kidney. — Endocrine III — Pregnancy & Placental Physiology lecture"},{"q":"How many umbilical arteries are normally present?","choices":["One","Four","Two","Three"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: There are normally two umbilical arteries (carrying deoxygenated blood to the placenta) and one umbilical vein. — Endocrine III — Pregnancy & Placental Physiology lecture"},{"q":"The umbilical vein carries:","choices":["Venous blood to the mother","Oxygenated blood to the fetus","Deoxygenated blood to the placenta","Mixed blood"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: The single umbilical vein carries oxygenated, nutrient-rich blood from placenta to fetus — opposite the arteries. — Endocrine III — Pregnancy & Placental Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Which placental hormone contributes to maternal insulin resistance?","choices":["hCG","Human placental lactogen (hPL)","Oxytocin","Prolactin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: Human placental lactogen (hPL) induces maternal insulin resistance to shunt glucose to the fetus, and can precipitate gestational diabetes. — Endocrine III — Pregnancy & Placental Physiology lecture"},{"q":"The purpose of maternal insulin resistance during pregnancy is to:","choices":["Preserve glucose for the fetus","Increase maternal fat loss only","Lower fetal glucose","Prevent placental growth"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: Maternal insulin resistance preserves circulating glucose for transfer to the fetus, which relies on maternal glucose for growth. — Endocrine III — Pregnancy & Placental Physiology lecture"},{"q":"By approximately the end of the first trimester, progesterone production shifts primarily from the:","choices":["Pituitary to placenta","Corpus luteum to placenta","Placenta to corpus luteum","Ovary to adrenal gland"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: By the end of the first trimester, progesterone production shifts from the corpus luteum to the placenta (the luteoplacental shift). — Endocrine III — Pregnancy & Placental Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Which hormone is MOST responsible for maintaining the endometrium during pregnancy?","choices":["FSH","LH","Progesterone","Estrogen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: Progesterone is the key hormone maintaining the endometrium (and quieting the uterus) throughout pregnancy. — Endocrine III — Pregnancy & Placental Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Estrogen during pregnancy contributes primarily to:","choices":["Uterine growth and breast duct development","Decreased uterine blood flow","Milk production","Ovulation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: Estrogen in pregnancy promotes uterine growth and breast duct development, preparing tissues for delivery and lactation. — Endocrine III — Pregnancy & Placental Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Relaxin primarily causes:","choices":["Milk ejection","Relaxation of pelvic ligaments and cervical softening","Increased uterine contractions","Increased progesterone secretion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: Relaxin softens the cervix and loosens pelvic ligaments/pubic symphysis to prepare the birth canal. — Endocrine III — Pregnancy & Placental Physiology lecture"},{"q":"A woman with low progesterone during early pregnancy is at increased risk for:","choices":["Diabetes insipidus","Acromegaly","Hyperthyroidism","Miscarriage"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: Low progesterone in early pregnancy removes uterine support, raising miscarriage risk — hence progesterone supplementation in some patients. — Endocrine III — Pregnancy & Placental Physiology lecture"},{"q":"During pregnancy, maternal blood volume generally:","choices":["Remains unchanged","Increases","Decreases","Doubles immediately"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: Maternal blood volume increases (~40–50%) during pregnancy to supply the uteroplacental unit and buffer delivery blood loss. — Endocrine III — Pregnancy & Placental Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Cardiac output during pregnancy:","choices":["Decreases","Stops increasing after implantation","Increases","Does not change"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: Cardiac output increases in pregnancy (higher heart rate and stroke volume) to meet the added circulatory demand. — Endocrine III — Pregnancy & Placental Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Maternal GFR during pregnancy:","choices":["Increases","Decreases","Remains unchanged","Falls after the first trimester"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: Maternal GFR increases due to elevated renal plasma flow, which is why creatinine tends to run lower in pregnancy. — Endocrine III — Pregnancy & Placental Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Maternal minute ventilation generally:","choices":["Decreases","Remains unchanged","Increases","Stops during labor"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: Minute ventilation increases (mainly tidal volume) under progesterone's stimulus, producing a mild compensated respiratory alkalosis. — Endocrine III — Pregnancy & Placental Physiology lecture"},{"q":"The period of greatest susceptibility to teratogens is:","choices":["Third trimester","Second trimester","First trimester (organogenesis)","Labor"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: The first trimester (organogenesis) is the period of greatest teratogen susceptibility, when organs are forming. — Endocrine III — Pregnancy & Placental Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Which trimester is characterized by rapid fetal growth and maternal awareness of fetal movement?","choices":["Second","First","Third","Postpartum"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: The second trimester features rapid fetal growth and quickening — the mother's first awareness of fetal movement. — Endocrine III — Pregnancy & Placental Physiology lecture"},{"q":"The third trimester is especially important for:","choices":["Implantation","Organogenesis","Lung maturation and weight gain","Fertilization"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: The third trimester is critical for lung maturation (surfactant) and fetal weight gain in preparation for birth. — Endocrine III — Pregnancy & Placental Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Normal maternal weight gain during pregnancy is approximately:","choices":["5-10 pounds","25-35 pounds","10-15 pounds","45-60 pounds"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: Normal total maternal weight gain is roughly 25–35 pounds for a woman of typical pre-pregnancy weight. — Endocrine III — Pregnancy & Placental Physiology lecture"},{"q":"A pregnant patient develops gestational diabetes because of increased:","choices":["Oxytocin","Prolactin","Human placental lactogen","Insulin sensitivity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: Gestational diabetes arises largely from human placental lactogen driving insulin resistance to favor fetal glucose supply. — Endocrine III — Pregnancy & Placental Physiology lecture"},{"q":"A woman is 8 weeks pregnant. Which hormone is primarily responsible for preventing degeneration of the corpus luteum?","choices":["hCG","LH","Progesterone","Prolactin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: At 8 weeks, hCG from the syncytiotrophoblast prevents corpus luteum degeneration, maintaining progesterone until the placenta takes over. — Endocrine III — Pregnancy & Placental Physiology lecture"},{"q":"Which hormone increases dramatically near term and contributes to uterine contractions?","choices":["Oxytocin","hCG","FSH","Prolactin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: Oxytocin rises near term and drives strong uterine contractions during labor, amplified by positive feedback. — Endocrine III — Parturition & Lactation lecture"},{"q":"The initiation of labor is associated with:","choices":["Decreased estrogen-to-progesterone ratio","Decreased oxytocin receptor expression","Decreased prostaglandin production","Increased estrogen-to-progesterone ratio"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: Labor onset is associated with an increased estrogen-to-progesterone ratio, which raises uterine excitability and oxytocin sensitivity. — Endocrine III — Parturition & Lactation lecture"},{"q":"Which hormone helps increase uterine sensitivity to oxytocin near term?","choices":["Progesterone","FSH","Estrogen","LH"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: Estrogen upregulates oxytocin receptors near term, increasing uterine sensitivity to oxytocin. Progesterone does the opposite. — Endocrine III — Parturition & Lactation lecture"},{"q":"Prostaglandins contribute to labor by:","choices":["Relaxing the uterus","Increasing uterine contractions and cervical ripening","Decreasing cervical dilation","Inhibiting oxytocin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: Prostaglandins promote uterine contractions and cervical ripening (softening/dilation), working alongside oxytocin in labor. — Endocrine III — Parturition & Lactation lecture"},{"q":"During labor, cervical stretch stimulates:","choices":["Decreased oxytocin","Decreased prostaglandins","Negative feedback","Positive feedback"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: Cervical stretch stimulates a positive feedback loop — the Ferguson reflex — driving more oxytocin and stronger contractions. — Endocrine III — Parturition & Lactation lecture"},{"q":"The positive feedback loop during labor involves:","choices":["Oxytocin → Decreased contractions","Progesterone → Increased cervical rigidity","Estrogen → Decreased oxytocin","Cervical stretch → Oxytocin → More contractions → More cervical stretch"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: Labor's positive feedback: cervical stretch → oxytocin → more contractions → more stretch, escalating until delivery. — Endocrine III — Parturition & Lactation lecture"},{"q":"The first stage of labor primarily consists of:","choices":["Cervical dilation","Delivery of the infant","Placental delivery","Milk production"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: The first stage of labor is cervical dilation and effacement, from onset until the cervix is fully dilated. — Endocrine III — Parturition & Lactation lecture"},{"q":"The second stage of labor ends with:","choices":["Placental delivery","Milk ejection","Delivery of the infant","Cervical dilation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: The second stage ends with delivery of the infant, spanning full dilation to birth. — Endocrine III — Parturition & Lactation lecture"},{"q":"The third stage of labor involves:","choices":["Uterine involution","Placental delivery","Breastfeeding","Ovulation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: The third stage involves delivery of the placenta after the baby is born. — Endocrine III — Parturition & Lactation lecture"},{"q":"Which hormone is routinely administered clinically to reduce postpartum hemorrhage?","choices":["FSH","hCG","Prolactin","Oxytocin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: Oxytocin is given clinically after delivery to contract the uterus and reduce postpartum hemorrhage. — Endocrine III — Parturition & Lactation lecture"},{"q":"Milk production is stimulated primarily by:","choices":["Estrogen","Prolactin","Progesterone","Oxytocin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: Prolactin (anterior pituitary) stimulates milk production/synthesis in the alveolar cells. — Endocrine III — Parturition & Lactation lecture"},{"q":"Milk ejection is stimulated primarily by:","choices":["Oxytocin","hCG","LH","Prolactin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: Oxytocin (posterior pituitary) drives milk ejection (let-down) by contracting myoepithelial cells — distinct from prolactin's production role. — Endocrine III — Parturition & Lactation lecture"},{"q":"Which pituitary lobe secretes prolactin?","choices":["Hypothalamus","Pineal gland","Posterior pituitary","Anterior pituitary"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: Prolactin is secreted by the anterior pituitary. (Oxytocin, by contrast, comes from the posterior pituitary.) — Endocrine III — Parturition & Lactation lecture"},{"q":"Which pituitary lobe releases oxytocin?","choices":["Adrenal cortex","Posterior pituitary","Thyroid","Anterior pituitary"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: Oxytocin is released from the posterior pituitary, where it is stored after hypothalamic synthesis. — Endocrine III — Parturition & Lactation lecture"},{"q":"Oxytocin causes contraction of:","choices":["Leydig cells","Granulosa cells","Myoepithelial cells","Alveolar epithelial cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: Oxytocin contracts myoepithelial cells surrounding the alveoli, squeezing milk into the ducts (let-down). — Endocrine III — Parturition & Lactation lecture"},{"q":"Suckling stimulates increased secretion of:","choices":["Estrogen only","Both prolactin and oxytocin","Prolactin only","Oxytocin only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: Suckling stimulates both prolactin (more milk production) and oxytocin (milk ejection) — a combined neuroendocrine reflex. — Endocrine III — Parturition & Lactation lecture"},{"q":"The major stimulus for prolactin release is:","choices":["High progesterone","Estrogen withdrawal","hCG","Suckling"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: Suckling is the major stimulus for prolactin release, sustaining milk production through the suckling reflex. — Endocrine III — Parturition & Lactation lecture"},{"q":"During breastfeeding, prolactin suppresses:","choices":["TSH","Cortisol","GnRH","ACTH"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: Prolactin suppresses GnRH during breastfeeding, lowering LH/FSH and often suppressing ovulation (lactational amenorrhea). — Endocrine III — Parturition & Lactation lecture"},{"q":"Suppression of GnRH during breastfeeding results in:","choices":["Increased progesterone","Decreased LH and FSH secretion","Increased ovulation","Increased estrogen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: GnRH suppression lowers LH and FSH secretion, which is why ovulation is often suppressed during lactation. — Endocrine III — Parturition & Lactation lecture"},{"q":"This temporary suppression of ovulation during breastfeeding is called:","choices":["Menopause","Lactational amenorrhea","Dysmenorrhea","Hyperprolactinemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: This breastfeeding-related suppression of ovulation is called lactational amenorrhea. — Endocrine III — Parturition & Lactation lecture"},{"q":"A woman has normal prolactin levels but no milk ejection. Which hormone is MOST likely deficient?","choices":["Oxytocin","FSH","Estrogen","LH"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: Normal prolactin but absent milk ejection points to an oxytocin deficiency, since oxytocin drives let-down. — Endocrine III — Parturition & Lactation lecture"},{"q":"A woman has adequate oxytocin but cannot produce milk. Which hormone is deficient?","choices":["Prolactin","Relaxin","hCG","Estrogen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: Adequate oxytocin but no milk production points to a prolactin deficiency, since prolactin drives milk synthesis. — Endocrine III — Parturition & Lactation lecture"},{"q":"Which hormone primarily maintains pregnancy?","choices":["Oxytocin","LH","FSH","Progesterone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: Progesterone is the principal hormone maintaining pregnancy by sustaining the endometrium and quieting the uterus. — Endocrine III — Parturition & Lactation lecture"},{"q":"Which hormone is responsible for maintaining the corpus luteum during early pregnancy?","choices":["Relaxin","hCG","Prolactin","Oxytocin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: hCG maintains the corpus luteum during early pregnancy so progesterone output continues until the placenta takes over. — Endocrine III — Parturition & Lactation lecture"},{"q":"The placenta eventually becomes the primary source of:","choices":["Progesterone and estrogen","LH","FSH","Testosterone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: By later pregnancy the placenta becomes the primary source of progesterone and estrogen, replacing the corpus luteum. — Endocrine III — Parturition & Lactation lecture"},{"q":"Which hormone prepares the uterus for implantation before pregnancy occurs?","choices":["Prolactin","hCG","Progesterone","Oxytocin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: Progesterone prepares the uterus for implantation by creating the secretory endometrium before pregnancy is established. — Endocrine III — Parturition & Lactation lecture"},{"q":"A patient has complete failure of LH secretion. Which process would be MOST directly impaired?","choices":["Milk production","Implantation","Lactation","Ovulation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: Complete LH failure most directly impairs ovulation, since the LH surge is the ovulatory trigger. — Endocrine III — Parturition & Lactation lecture"},{"q":"A patient receives an oxytocin infusion during labor. The expected response is:","choices":["Decreased uterine contractions","Increased uterine contractions","Suppressed prostaglandin production","Decreased cervical dilation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: An oxytocin infusion increases uterine contractions — its intended clinical effect for induction/augmentation of labor. — Endocrine III — Parturition & Lactation lecture"},{"q":"A patient has destruction of the anterior pituitary after childbirth (Sheehan syndrome). Which finding is MOST likely?","choices":["Increased prolactin","Failure of milk production","Increased FSH","Excess oxytocin release"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: Sheehan syndrome (postpartum anterior pituitary infarction) causes failure of milk production, since prolactin comes from the anterior pituitary. — Endocrine III — Parturition & Lactation lecture"},{"q":"A newborn begins suckling immediately after birth. Which sequence correctly describes the endocrine response?","choices":["Suckling → ↑ hCG → Lactation","Suckling → ↓ Prolactin → ↓ Oxytocin","Suckling → ↑ Oxytocin → Milk ejection AND ↑ Prolactin → Milk production","Suckling → ↑ Progesterone → Milk production"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: Suckling triggers oxytocin → milk ejection AND prolactin → milk production — the combined reflex that sustains breastfeeding. — Endocrine III — Parturition & Lactation lecture"}]};
